WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
Speaker 1: I'm going to start today with the the&nbsp;
prototypical story really that is an objectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:11.600
important story to understand what's going on in&nbsp;
the economy and that almost no one cares about and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.840
almost no one will watch or engage with. But it&nbsp;
doesn't matter. It's actually an important story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.840
So we're going to start with it in the story is&nbsp;
that the Federal Reserve has raised interest rates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:25.760
by what's called sometimes 50 basis points or&nbsp;
half a percent. When they say 50, what they mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:33.680
is 0.50%. This is the biggest increase in 22 years&nbsp;
since the year 2000. And so much of what's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:40.080
on today that is flippantly mentioned and in so&nbsp;
many of these 2022 debates that you can tell the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:45.520
candidates don't really understand comes down&nbsp;
to understanding what is the impact of raising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.120
raising or lowering the federal funds rate. And so&nbsp;
that's what we're going to talk about. And before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:57.200
I get into the details. Yesterday we watched and&nbsp;
we livestreamed the 2022 Pennsylvania Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:03.440
Senate primary debate. This is the one with Mehmet&nbsp;
Oz and Dave McCormick and Carla Sands and Kathy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.960
Barnett, etc.. Bartos, I think is the fifth guy.&nbsp;
Now I'm actually remembering their names and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:14.480
were asked, all of them asked about inflation and&nbsp;
balancing the budgets and the economy and so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:21.840
different things. And all five of them expressed&nbsp;
no knowledge whatsoever of how these basic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:27.680
tools that are available to different government&nbsp;
entities work. With the exception of at one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:34.240
Mehmet Oz mentioned raising the rate to lower&nbsp;
inflation, and that was true. But then he moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.240
on and went beyond that. So let's jump into the&nbsp;
details and then we'll explain what's going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:45.680
Yahoo! Finance Report Fed Raises Interest Rates&nbsp;
0.5%. Largest move since the year 2000. Good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.840
This is the short term interest rate.&nbsp;
This is an effort to tamp down on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.960
inflationary pressures weighing on Americans.&nbsp;
The central bank suggests that it will further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.720
raise borrowing costs throughout this year&nbsp;
as it attempts to undo its pandemic era&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:09.040
easy money policies. The policy setting Federal&nbsp;
Open Market Committee also detailed plans on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:15.040
unwinding its nearly $9 trillion balance sheet.&nbsp;
The raising of rates a half a percent is the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.000
aggressive increase in a single meeting since&nbsp;
May of 2000. Over the last two decades, the Fed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.960
has opted to raise interest rates in increments&nbsp;
of a quarter of a percent only. So let's talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:33.920
about this. How how does the rate goes up and&nbsp;
inflation goes down? How does that work? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:41.600
when you raise the federal funds rate, you make&nbsp;
it more expensive for banks to borrow money. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.960
it's more expensive for banks to borrow money,&nbsp;
they in turn make it more expensive for people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:52.720
like you and me and businesses to borrow money.&nbsp;
When it's more expensive for us to borrow money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:03:00.000
We do less borrowing. And when you see the ways&nbsp;
in which this is impactful, it will start to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.520
intuitive sense. So if it becomes more expensive&nbsp;
to get a mortgage, that's one form of borrowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.720
that makes a $300,000 house more&nbsp;
expensive. What I mean by that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:17.200
the house might be $300,000 either way, but if&nbsp;
my mortgage on it is five and a quarter percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:23.200
instead of 5% now that same $300,000 house is&nbsp;
more expensive for me. So instead of spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.320
300,000 on a house, I might only spend&nbsp;
290. So that pushes down people's budgets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.040
and it can push down housing prices or at least&nbsp;
slow down the increase. That's one component.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.880
Vehicles. Car loans. If car loans are more&nbsp;
expensive, you might be less likely to get a car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:45.360
loan. Or if you do, you'll get a cheaper car. That&nbsp;
pushes down on price increases, it discourages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:51.840
spending and it can lower inflation. Now, many&nbsp;
people will say, but, David, it can also slow down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:58.400
the economy. Well, how how does that work? Same&nbsp;
principle. One of the ways that an economy grows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:04.160
is by people investing in their own businesses.&nbsp;
Oftentimes, people, times people borrow to invest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:10.320
in their business. Imagine that. I want to start&nbsp;
a shoe business and I need a factory and I need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.840
to borrow in order to build that factory. Well,&nbsp;
if it's more expensive to borrow, I'm less likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:22.320
to do it, or I'm less likely to borrow as much.&nbsp;
Meaning that instead of maybe having $400,000 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:27.600
build my factory, which would go to contractors&nbsp;
and lawyers and insurance and local businesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:35.280
now I only have $300,000, so I'm slowing down&nbsp;
economic growth in that way. It always is the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:42.720
that every one of these decisions is a double&nbsp;
edged sword. It is always the case. And so very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:48.560
often you see hyperpartisan discussions where&nbsp;
we shouldn't raise rates because that will slow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.920
down the economy. Right. But it could also lower&nbsp;
inflation, which many of you claim to want to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:57.760
Well, we shouldn't lower rates because&nbsp;
it makes it too easy to borrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.120
and therefore it will cause inflation. But, yes,&nbsp;
that that's the that's the way it works. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.760
the double edged sword. And the whole strategy,&nbsp;
the whole trick of it is to keep these things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:13.440
in balance. One other way in which raising&nbsp;
the funds rate discourages spending and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:20.720
it can both lower inflation but slow down the&nbsp;
economy. If if anybody has a savings account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.480
I was going to call it a high yield savings&nbsp;
account, but there's very little high yield about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:29.760
it. Many so-called high yield savings accounts&nbsp;
over the last couple of years at the most pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.680
about half a percent. There's a number of high&nbsp;
yield savings account where you can earn 0.5%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:39.920
Not a great incentive to just store your money&nbsp;
because it's lower than inflation. If the federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:47.360
funds rate goes up half a point and thus high&nbsp;
yield savings accounts pay you more interest, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:53.600
might lean a little more towards now a more. Okay,&nbsp;
just saving the money and earning the interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:57.680
because the interest rate is a little bit&nbsp;
higher and instead of putting that money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:02.960
into the economy, you just save it. So that that's&nbsp;
another aspect to this. Every single one of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:08.880
things can have multiple effects, and that is the&nbsp;
importance of raising the federal funds rate. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:15.040
we'll see what the impact is and we will see what&nbsp;
forthcoming additional increases there are. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:21.520
we've been we've been warned to expect more. I&nbsp;
hope that that's clear, or at least relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.800
simple. If it's not, let me know and also let&nbsp;
me know if these types of segments are useful.

